messages.content:
Chrome was holding ~2.8 GB across many tabs on an 8 GB machine with only 0.6 GB free, causing heavy paging and slowness. The system itself is healthy; the load is application-driven.
Commands used:
- Get-Process | Sort-Object WorkingSet64 -Descending | Select-Object -First 5 Name, @{N='MB';E={[int]($_.WorkingSet64/1MB)}}
- Get-CimInstance Win32_OperatingSystem | Select-Object @{N='FreeGB';E={[math]::Round($_.FreePhysicalMemory/1MB,1)}}, @{N='TotalGB';E={[math]::Round($_.TotalVisibleMemorySize/1MB,1)}}
Recommendation: Close unused Chrome tabs or install a tab-suspender, and consider upgrading to 16 GB RAM for comfortable multitasking.

The user temp folder held ~3 GB of junk. Clearing it (safe, in-use files are skipped) freed space, bringing C: back to 11 GB free.
Commands used:
- Get-ChildItem $env:TEMP -Recurse -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Measure-Object Length -Sum | Select-Object @{N='MB';E={[int]($_.Sum/1MB)}}
- Remove-Item "$env:TEMP\*" -Recurse -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
- Get-PSDrive C | Select-Object @{N='FreeGB';E={[int]($_.Free/1GB)}}
Recommendation: Run Disk Cleanup / Storage Sense for Windows Update leftovers to reclaim more.

The temp folder held ~2.6 GB. Clearing it freed most of it; a couple of in-use files were safely skipped (that warning is expected). C: now has 9 GB free.
Commands used:
- Get-ChildItem $env:TEMP -Recurse -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Measure-Object Length -Sum | Select-Object @{N='MB';E={[int]($_.Sum/1MB)}}
- Remove-Item "$env:TEMP\*" -Recurse -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
- Get-PSDrive C | Select-Object @{N='FreeGB';E={[int]($_.Free/1GB)}}
Recommendation: Close Chrome and re-run to clear the last locked temp files, or use Disk Cleanup for Windows Update leftovers.
